{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg73MS5n19ch7E1KUWHZV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The default `max_connections` in PostgreSQL is **100**.\n\n`initdb` picks that value when it creates the cluster. It tries 100 first and drops to a lower value if the system's shared memory or semaphore limits can't support it. On most modern systems you get 100.\n\nSome related defaults:\n- `superuser_reserved_connections` is 3, so regular users effectively get 97.\n- Changing `max_connections` requires a server restart.\n- Hot standbys must set `max_connections` to at least the primary's value.\n- Managed services such as RDS, Cloud SQL, and Azure often override the default, usually by scaling it with instance memory.\n\nTo check the current value, run `SHOW max_connections;`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6841,"cache_read_input_tokens":11227,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6841},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"653a6ce7-31dc-473c-ba5d-78023c8c516f","uuid":"236592e5-ab05-4d8e-9c47-3428cc62e465","timestamp":"2026-10-17T02:44:19.996Z","request_id":"req_011Cg73MRs8JprUq79LJa8Bd"}
